 # Routing capacity and request queues
@@ -21,6 +21,44 @@ Inspect the Activity view and logs when latency grows. A queue that never drains
 usually means the command, proxy, or health check is wrong; see
 `guides/model-runtime/troubleshooting-model-wont-load`.
 
+## Priority-shaped concurrency limit
+
+`models.*.priorityConcurrencyLimit` replaces `concurrencyLimit` for one model
+and applies the cap per request priority instead of to the total. Priority is
+the request-body `priority` field, the vLLM convention: lower is more urgent, a
+request that states none is 0, and a negative value is more urgent than 0. This
+is not `routing.scheduler.settings.fifo.priority`, which ranks queued model
+swaps and is higher-is-first.
+
+```yaml
+models:
+  gpt-oss:
+    priorityConcurrencyLimit: 24
+```
+
+A request carrying priority `p` is admitted when fewer than
+`priorityConcurrencyLimit` requests are already in flight for the model at
+priority `p` or lower. In flight means admitted and not yet finished: queued,
+waiting on a swap, or being served. Traffic less urgent than a request never
+blocks it. Each priority level can use the whole cap on its own, so total in
+flight can exceed it. With a cap of 24, twenty-four in-flight requests at
+priority 0 shed a new request at priority 0 and at priority 50, but admit one
+at priority -1.
+
+A non-integer priority counts as 0, and so does a form upload, which cannot
+carry the field. The priority is read after request-body filters run, so a
+server-side `setParams` stamp sets it too and you can assign urgency to a client
+that does not send one.
+
+Use this with a backend that ranks work the same way, such as vLLM started with
+`--scheduling-policy priority`, which accepts the same integer field. The router
+decides whether to accept a request. The backend decides which of its in-flight
+work runs first.
+
+Rejection is unchanged: an immediate HTTP 429 with `Retry-After: 1`, the same
+response `concurrencyLimit` produces. When both keys are set for a model,
+`priorityConcurrencyLimit` wins and `concurrencyLimit` does not apply.
+
